ests, from excitation design to flight tests, including coordinating with the .</p></li><li><p style="min-height:1.5em">Model Identification: Identify rigid-body, aerodynamic, propulsion and actuator dynamics using frequency and time-domain methods, including closed-loop identification on vehicles flown under a controller.</p></li><li><p style="min-height:1.5em">Propulsion &amp; Actuators: Characterize motors, ESCs, propellers and control surfaces, including bandwidth, delays, saturation, and thermal and battery effects.</p></li><li><p style="min-height:1.5em">Identification Pipeline: Build and maintain an automated, reproducible pipeline from flight logs to identified model. This covers ingestion, data-quality checks, estimation, validation and reporting.</p></li><li><p style="min-height:1.5em">Model Validation: Validate models against held-out flight data, quantify uncertainty, and make sure each model is fit for purpose: controller design, simulation, or training learned G&amp;C.</p></li><li><p style="min-height:1.5em">New Airframes: Extend the capability from multirotors to control-surface multirotors, fixed wings, tail-sitters and missiles.</p></li><li><p style="min-height:1.5em">Simulation &amp; G&amp;C Support: Deliver models into simulation, SITL and HITL, and work closely with control, guidance and embedded engineers to close the loop between models and flight results.</p></li><li><p style="min-height:1.5em">Mentorship: Depending on seniority, support the team by mentoring junior engineers and interns.</p></li></ul><div style="min-height:1.2em;margin-top:0px;margin-bottom:0px"> </div><div style="min-height:1.2em;margin-top:0px;margin-bottom:0px"> </div><h3><strong>Candidate Requirements</strong></h3><ul style="min-height:1.5em"><li><p style="min-height:1.5em">Educational Background: A strong academic record with a degree in aerospace, control, mechanical engineering or a related STEM field. A practical track record matters more than the specific degree.</p>